A thorough four-phase investigation of the live network found no fault at any layer. Phase 1 (topology-wide reachability) confirmed all IP-level paths between every host pair return 0% packet loss. Phase A infrastructure sweep found no ACL/firewall drop rules, no ARP anomalies, no duplicate MAC addresses, and all interfaces up and correctly addressed. Phase B OSPF snapshot confirmed all six FRR routers (router_core_1, router_core_2, router_core_3, switch_dist_1_1, switch_dist_2_1, switch_server_access) are healthy — vtysh responsive, all sockets present, every neighbor in Full adjacency state, and nine OSPF routes per router. Phase D service snapshot directly tested the DNS complaint: both end-hosts (host_1_1_1_1 and host_2_1_1_1) successfully resolved all four internal names (web0.local, web1.local, web2.local, web3.local) to the correct IP addresses via the DNS server at 10.200.0.2, and all HTTP requests to those names returned HTTP 200 in under 5 ms. The pressure sweep found no CPU exhaustion, no stressed sockets, and no crashed daemons. The symptom described in helpdesk ticket #8842 — complete internal name resolution failure — is not reproducible on the current live network state. The network's DNS, routing, and application layers are all operating normally. Root cause: no anomaly detected; the reported symptom is unconfirmed by any current or historical evidence in the network.
Fix: No corrective action required. If the user continues to experience failures, verify that their client device's resolver is pointed at 10.200.0.2 and that no local host-file override is shadowing internal names.
